[0.128, "o", "\u001b[?2026h\u001b[?25l\u001b[H\u001b[2;35r\u001b[14S\u001b[r\u001b[H\u001b[6G\u001b[38;2;248;248;242m\u001b[2m  1 \u001b[22m# Research: Expense Splitting API\u001b[39m\u001b[K\r\u001b[14B\u001b[38;2;78;186;101m⏺\r\u001b[1B\u001b[38;2;153;153;153m  ⎿  \u001b[39mAdded\u001b[12G\u001b[1m4\u001b[14G\u001b[22mlines,\u001b[21Gremoved\u001b[29G\u001b[1m3\u001b[31G\u001b[22mlines\r\u001b[1B \u001b[3G   \u001b[38;2;248;248;242m\u001b[2m 57 \u001b[22m   otherwise falls back to the pure-Python implementation (exact, slower), so exactness never\r\u001b[1B\u001b[39m     \u001b[38;2;248;248;242m\u001b[2m 58 \u001b[22m   depends on fixed-width integers.\u001b[39m\u001b[K\r\u001b[5C\u001b[1B\u001b[38;2;248;248;242m\u001b[2m 59 \u001b[22m - **Rationale**: FR-009 requires the true minimum; greedy matching of largest debtor with\r\u001b[1B\u001b[39m \u001b[3G   \u001b[38;2;220;90;90m\u001b[48;2;61;1;0m 60 -\u001b[38;2;248;248;242m  largest creditor is not optimal (e.g. A+5, B−5, C+7, D−7 can be split poorly by other                                                              \r\u001b[1B\u001b[49m\u001b[39m     \u001b[38;2;220;90;90m\u001b[48;2;61;1;0m 61 -\u001b[38;2;248;248;242m  inputs such as A+3, B+4, C−3, D−4 orderings). The spec caps groups at 20 members, keeping                         "]
[0.001, "o", "                                 \r\u001b[5C\u001b[1B\u001b[38;2;220;90;90m 62 -\u001b[38;2;248;248;242m  2^20 feasible.                                                                                                                                     \r\u001b[5C\u001b[1B\u001b[38;2;80;200;80m\u001b[48;2;2;40;0m 60 +\u001b[38;2;248;248;242m  largest creditor is not optimal. Counterexample (verified by brute force): balances                                                                \r\u001b[5C\u001b[1B\u001b[38;2;80;200;80m 61 +\u001b[38;2;248;248;242m  −7, −8, +2, +6, +7 — greedy uses 4 transfers, optimum is 3 ({−7, +7} and {−8, +2, +6}).                                                            \r\u001b[5C\u001b[1B\u001b[38;2;80;200;80m 62 +\u001b[38;2;248;248;242m  This case MUST be a regression test. The spec caps groups at 20 members, keeping 2^20                                                              \r\u001b[5C\u001b[1B\u001b[38;2;80;200;80m 63 +\u001b[38;2;248;248;242m  states feasible.                                                                                                       "]
[0.001, "o", "                            \r\u001b[5C\u001b[1B\u001b[49m\u001b[2m 64 \u001b[22m - **Alternatives considered**: Greedy (≤ k−1 transfers, not minimum). Backtracking DFS\r\u001b[5C\u001b[1B\u001b[2m 65 \u001b[22m   (fast typically, exponential worst case without a clear bound). Pure-Python DP (correct\r\u001b[5C\u001b[1B\u001b[2m 66 \u001b[22m   but 1.85 s worst case).\r\u001b[2B\u001b[38;2;153;153;153m \u001b[3G\u001b[39mCreating\u001b[12Gcontracts\u001b[22Gdirectory\r\u001b[1B\u001b[38;2;153;153;153m  ⎿  $ cd /Users/maya/Workspace/sdd-bake-off/frameworks/speckit; mkdir -p specs/001-expense-splitting-api/contracts\r\u001b[2B\u001b[38;2;215;119;87m✳\u001b[3GMisting… \u001b[38;2;153;153;153m(running PreToolUse hook · 1m 42s · ↓\u001b[50G8.1k tokens)\r\u001b[1B  ⎿  Tip: Use /btw to ask a quick side question without interrupting Claude's current work\u001b[39m\u001b[40;1H\u001b[38;3H\u001b[?25h\u001b[?2026l"]
